support\nL647@P13: of ψ such that b = a mod IΩ. By Eq. 2.3, we know that the minimum of ΣK(IΩ)\nL648@P13: with respect to the Euclidean norm is bounded from below by √\nL649@P13: d · N (IΩ)\nL650@P13: √\nL651@P13: 1/d =\nL652@P13: d · q\nL653@P13: t/d. If there were two solutions a 6= a0 ∈ OK such that a = a0 mod IΩ, then\nL654@P13: the element a − a\nL655@P13: 0 would lie in IΩ and its Euclidean norm with respect to the\nL656@P13: canonical embedding would be bounded above by 2B < √\nL657@P13: d· q\nL658@P13: t/d ≤ λ1(ΣK(IΩ)),\nL659@P13: leading to a contradiction. Hence, the PV-Knap problem is well defined.\nL660@P13: 8 Even though they originally called it the partial Fourier recovery problem.\nL661@P13-14: 14We can also define a decision variant of PV-Knap in the natural way. Given Ω\nL662@P14: and b+IΩ, one has to decide whether b was defined as in the problem’s definition\nL663@P14: above or if it was sampled uniformly at random.\nL664@P14: Whereas in the above definition PV-Knap is defined over OK, the prob\u0002lem is in some works (e.g. [HPS+14, HS15, DHSS20]) defined over the cyclic\nL665@P14: ring Z[X]/(XN − 1) for some prime integer N. We recall its concrete formu\u0002lation in Appendix B. Note that those rings are closely connected to prime\nL666@P14: cyclotomic number fields as the polynomial XN − 1 factors into two particular\nL667@P14: irreducible polynomials. More precisely, we have XN − 1 = (X − 1)·ΦN (X) and\nL668@P14: thus there exists an injective ring morphism from Z[X]/(XN − 1) to Z[X]/(X −\nL669@P14: 1)×Z[X]/ΦN (X). Using this morphism, one can transform a PV-Knap instance\nL670@P14: over the cyclic ring into a PV-Knap instance over Z[X]/ΦN (X), i.e., the ring of\nL671@P14: integers of a cyclotomic fields. A solution to PV-Knap in Z[X]/ΦN (X) can then\nL672@P14: be lifted back to Z[X]/(XN −1) by guessing the last coordinate in Z[X]/(X −1).\nL673@P14: Hence, even though the results of Section 4 are formulated for number fields, they\n--------