on K/KI is always Galois, even if K/Q is not\nL729@P15: (Lemma 2.2). The generalization to non-prime ideals requires more work. The\nL730@P15: main difficulty of this generalization lies in proving the following lemma.\nL731@P15: Lemma 3.3. Let K/L be a Galois extension of number fields. Let I be an in\u0002tegral ideal of K whose prime factors are not ramified in K/L. If σ(I) = I for\nL732@P15: all σ ∈ Gal(K/L), then it holds that\nL733@P15: I = (I ∩ OL) · OK.\nL734@P15: Intuitively, this lemma means that when intersecting the ideal I with the\nL735@P15: subfield L, one loses no information on I, since it can be recovered simply by\nL736@P15: multiplying by OK again. This conveys the intuition that the short vectors of I\nL737@P15: should also be contained into the intersection I ∩ OL.\nL738@P15: Proof. Note that the inclusion I ⊇ (I ∩ OL) · OK always holds, even if I is\nL739@P15: divisible by ramified primes, or if σ(I) 6= I for some σ ∈ Gal(K/L). However, in\nL740@P15: the general case, this inclusion is usually not an equality: the set (I ∩ OL) · OK\nL741@P15: can be much sparser than I, hence losing information about I. In the rest of this\nL742@P15: proof, we focus on proving the reverse inclusion I ⊆ (I ∩ OL) · OK.\nL743@P15: First, we group the prime factors of I into groups of primes that are all\nL744@P15: above the same prime in OL. In other words, we write I =\nL745@P15: Q\nL746@P15: q prime of OL\nL747@P15: Iq,\nL748@P15: where Iq =\nL749@P15: Q\nL750@P15: pi prime of OK above q\nL751@P15: p\nL752@P15: αi\nL753@P15: i\nL754@P15: .\nL755@P15: Let us fix a prime ideal q in OL, which does not ramify in OK (recall that we\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/709.pdf)\nciteturn18view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.1 years ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 35; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view0\",\"lineno\":972}); Total lines: 1848\nL937@P17: Output: A solution 