36@P20: leading to kΣK(1/f0(X))k∞ ≤\nL1137@P20: 2\nL1138@P20: m and thus completing the proof. ut\nL1139@P20: We prove Lemma 4.1 in the following three steps.\nL1140@P20: Step 1: From Id-HBDD in I to Id-HSIVP in I\nL1141@P20: ∨. This reduction is well\u0002known for BDD and SIVP in their standard formulation and works for any\nL1142@P20: lattice, not only for ideal lattices. It corresponds to solving BDD in a lattice L\nL1143@P20: by using the so-called Babai’s rounding algorithm [Bab86], whose performance\nL1144@P20: can be assessed by looking at the size of the vectors of the dual basis of L\nL1145@P20: ∨\nL1146@P20: (see for instance [CDPR16, Claim 2.1]). For completeness, we detail out how\nL1147@P20: to proceed for the Hermite variant, and quantify the loss in the approximation\nL1148@P20: factor for this variant in the following.\nL1149@P20: Lemma 4.4 (Id-HBDD to Id-HSIVP). Let I be a fractional ideal of a number\nL1150@P20: field K of degree d. There is a (deterministic) polynomial-time reduction from γ1-\nL1151@P20: Id-HBDDK in I to γ2-Id-HSIVPK in I\nL1152@P20: ∨, for any 2γ2 < γ1.\nL1153@P20-21: 21Proof. Let I be an ideal lattice and let t ∈ SpanR(ΣK(I)) be an instance of γ1-\nL1154@P21: Id-HBDDK, with the promise that t = v + e with v ∈ ΣK(I) and kek ≤\nL1155@P21: 1/γ1 · det(ΣK(I))1/d. Further, assume that we are able to solve γ2-Id-HSIVPK\nL1156@P21: in the dual ideal I\nL1157@P21: ∨, i.e., we obtain d linearly independent vectors b∨\nL1158@P21: j ∈ ΣK(I\nL1159@P21: ∨)\nL1160@P21: for j ∈ {1, . . . , d} such that maxj kb\nL1161@P21: ∨\nL1162@P21: j\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2010/014/revision/1/download)\nciteturn18view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view0\",\"lineno\":409}); Total lines: 1739\nL390@P6: B = [b1, . . . , bn] for the same lattice such that µ(L([b1, . . . , bi])/kb\nL391@P6: ∗\nL392@P6: i\nL393@P6: k ≤ O(n\nL394@P6: 2\nL395@P6: ) for all i = 1, . . . , n.\nL396@P6: Lattice problems In this paper we are mostly concerned with the Closest Vector Problem (CVP) defined\nL397@P6: in the introduction: given a lattice basis B and a target vector t, find a lattice vector v ∈ L(B) that minimizes\nL398@P6: the distance kt − vk. Our results give algorithms for several other lattice problems like the Shortest Vector\nL399@P6: Problem (SVP), Shortest Independent Vectors Problem (SIVP), Subspace Avoiding Problem (SAP), the\nL400@P6: Generalized Closest Vector Problem (GCVP), and the Successive Minima Problem (SMP) considered in the\nL401@P6: lattice algorithms literature [BN09, Mic08]. The results for all problems other than CVP can be obtained\nL402@P6: obtained in a black-box way by (dimension preserving) reduction to CVP [Mic08], and we refer the reader\nL403@P6: to [GMSS99, BN09, Mic08] for details. Also, using our new algorithm to implement the SVP oracle within\nL404@P6: the block reduction algorithms of [GN08, Sch87], yields deterministic polynomial time approximations for\nL405@P6: SVP, SIVP, CVP, etc. within a factor 2O(n log log n/ log n). Previous polynomial time algorithms either used\nL406@P6: randomization, or achieved a weaker approximation factor 2O(n(log log n)\nL407@P6: 2/ log n)\nL408@P6: .\nL409@P6: For simplicity we assume that the input lattices B ∈ Z\nL410@P6: d×n and target vectors t ∈ Zd have integer entries\nL411@P6: with bit-size polynomial in the lattice dimension n, and that the number of entries d in each vector is also\nL412@P6: polynomial in n. This allows to express the complexity of lattice problems simply as a function of a single\nL413@P6: parameter, the lattice dimension n. All the results in this paper can be easily adapted to the general case\nL414@P6: by introducing an explicit bound log |bi,j | ≤ M on the size of the entries, and letting the time and space\nL415@P6: complexity bounds depend polynomially on M and d. We write f = O˜(g) when f(n) is bounded by g(n) up\nL416@P6: to polylogarithmic factors, i.e., f(n) ≤ logcg(n) · g(n) for some constant c and all sufficiently large n.\nL417@P6: Coding conventions In order to make the description of our algorithms both precise, but also intu\u0002itive and easy to understand and analyze, we have used pseudocode written whenever possible in a high\nL418@P6: level, mathematical/functional style. We use two different notations for variable assignments, x = . . . for\nL419@P6: immutable variables that do not change their values during the execution, and x ← . . . for destructive assign\u0002ment in stateful computations where the value of x is updated. Iteration is often expressed as tail recursion,\nL420@P6-7: 7in order to facilitate inductive proofs of correctness. Some of the loops are written in the form “for x ∈ A\nL421@P7: sorted by f(x)”, where x is a variable, A a set, and f(x) an integer or real valued function. This represents\nL422@P7: a loop over all elements of the set A, in order of nondecreasing value of f(x). Enumerating the elements\nL423@P7: of A in order of nondecreasing value of f(x) is either straightforward, or (when A is finite) it can be easily\nL424@P7: achieved using any efficient sorting algorithm running in O(|A| log |A|) time. The body of loops where the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2010/014/revision/1/download)\nciteturn18view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view0\",\"lineno\":1472}); Total lines: 1739\nL1446@P19: space O˜(2n). In order to bound the running time, we prove that the algorithm terminates after at most hn\nL1447@P19: iterations of the main loop. For each index j ∈ {1, . . . , 2\nL1448@P19: n}, let vj be a shortest vector in the corresponding\nL1449@P19: coset vj + 2Λ, and let ij be the index of the layer ijbn + Λ0containing vj . Assume without loss of generality\nL1450@P19: that ij ≥ 0. (If ij < 0, then replace vj with −vj ∈ vj + 2Λ.) Since vj is in span(Λ) = span(2Λ), it\nL1451@P19: must be kvjk ≤ µ(2Λ) = 2µ(Λ). By taking the component of vj in the direction of b\nL1452@P19: ∗\nL1453@P19: n\nL1454@P19: , we also get that\nL1455@P19: kvjk ≥ ij · kb\nL1456@P19: ∗\nL1457@P19: nk. Combining the two inequalities, we get that ij · kb\nL1458@P19: ∗\nL1459@P19: nk ≤ kvjk ≤ 2µ(Λ). This proves that vj\nL1460@P19: (or an equally short vector from the same coset) is found within the first ij ≤ 2µ(Λ)/kb\nL1461@P19: ∗\nL1462@P19: nk = hn iterations\nL1463@P19: of the main loop. So, by the time the algorithm enters iteration i = hn + 1, we have maxj kT[j]k ≤ 2µ(Λ),\nL1464@P19: and the algorithm terminates because i · kb\nL1465@P19: ∗\nL1466@P19: nk > hn · kb\nL1467@P19: ∗\nL1468@P19: nk = 2µ(Λ).\nL1469@P19: 5.2 Main Algorithm\nL1470@P19: A faster algorithm for Voronoi cell computation is obtained simply by replacing Algorithm 2 from Section 4\nL1471@P19: with Algorithm 4.\nL1472@P19: Theorem 5.4 There is a deterministic O˜(22n)-time and O˜(2n)-space algorithm that on input an n-dimensional\nL1473@P19: lattice Λ with basis B, outputs the relevant vectors of Λ.\nL1474@P19: Proof: The main function is the same VoronoiCell from Algorithm 3 already analyzed in Theorem 4.10,\nL1475@P19: but with Preprocess and VRevelant implemented as in Algorithm 4. The analysis is almost identical\nL1476@P19: to the one in the proof of Theorem 4.10, with the following differences. The preprocessing function is no\nL1477@P19: longer polynomial time, but by Lemma 5.1 it still runs within the O˜(22n)-time and O˜(2n)-space bounds.\nL1478@P19: Also, after preprocessing, the basis Bn satisfies hk = µ(L(b1, . . . , bi))/kb\nL1479@P19: ∗\nL1480@P19: i\nL1481@P19: k ≤ O(n\nL1482@P19: 2\nL1483@P19: ) for all i = 1, . . . , n. So,\nL1484@P19-20: 20by Lemma 5.3, each call to the new VRevelant function runs in O˜(hk · |Vk−1| · 2\nL1485@P20: k\nL1486@P20: ) = O˜(22k) time. The\nL1487@P20: rest of the running time analysis is similar to Theorem 4.10, and yields a O˜(22n) bound on the total running\nL1488@P20: time. The proof of correctness and space complexity bound is identical.\nL1489@P20: From the list of Voronoi relevant vectors, we immediately get a solution to many other lattice problems,\nL1490@P20: e.g., the shortest vector problem (SVP) can be solved simply by picking the shortest vector in the list of\nL1491@P20: lattice points describing the Voronoi cell, and the kissing number of the lattice can be computed as the\nL1492@P20: number of vectors in the list achieving the same length as the shortest vector in the lattice.\nL1493@P20: Corollary 5.5 There is a deterministic O˜(22n)-time algorithm to solve SVP, and to compute the kissing\nL1494@P20: number of a lattice.\nL1495@P20: The Voronoi relevant vectors can also be used with our CVPP algorithm to solve CVP.\nL1496@P20: Corollary 5.6 There is a deterministic O˜(22n)-time, O˜(2n)-space algorithm to solve CVP.\nL1497@P20: Proof: The algorithm is given by function CVP from Algorithm 3. By Theorem 5.4 the computation of\nL1498@P20: the Voronoi cell takes O˜(22n) time and O˜(2n) space. Once the Voronoi cell has been computed, the input\nL1499@P20: CVP instance is solved using the CVPP algorithm, which, by Theorem 4.2, runs in time O˜(22n).\nL1500@P20: Algorithms for other lattice problems, like SIVP, SAP, GCVP, SMP (see [BN09, Mic08]) can be obtained\nL1501@P20: by reduction to CVP.\n"
